Exercise 9's figure caption describes the ellipse x^2+4y^2=1 as having
"major axis 2, and minor axis 0.5." The semi-axes of this ellipse are 1
(along x) and 1/2 (along y), so the full axis lengths are 2 and 1. The
caption already states the major axis using the full length (2, not the
semi-axis 1), matching the convention used consistently elsewhere in this
file (e.g. exercise 10's "major axis 2, and minor axis roughly 0.64" for
9x^2+y^2<=1, whose full minor-axis length is 2/3). Exercise 9's minor axis
was left at the semi-axis value instead. Hand-fixed to "minor axis 1" to
match the file's own full-length convention; this defect is present
verbatim in OpenStax's own source caption, not introduced by conversion.

Exercise 11's figure caption lists the corners of the square region bounded
by y=x, y=-x, y=x+2, y=-x+2 as "the origin, (2, 0), (1, 1), and (negative
1, 1)." Solving the boundary lines pairwise gives vertices (0,0), (1,1),
(0,2), and (-1,1); (2,0) is not a vertex of this region (it lies only on
the line y=-x+2, not at an intersection of two boundary lines). Hand-fixed
the listed corner from (2, 0) to (0, 2). This defect is also present
verbatim in OpenStax's own source caption.

